      "type": "text",
      "text": "```json\n{\"unit_id\": \"Nubemhat\", \"choices\": [], \"reasoning\": \"Record 1, Nubemhat, follows the usual modern conventions for Nbw-m-ḥꜣ.t, 'gold is in front'. 'Nub' is the standard rendering of nbw 'gold', as in Nubkheperre and Nubnefer. 'Em' is the preposition m. 'Hat' is ḥꜣ.t 'front', with ꜣ shown as a. C1 Nb-m-ht ('Neb-em-het') is nb + m + ht. Its consonants n-b-m-h-t fit the spelling. But its first word is nb ('Neb', lord/all), not nbw ('Nub', gold), and its last word is ht ('het'), not ḥꜣ.t ('hat', front). Record 1 renders different words, so C1 is a different name. C2 Nb-mḥy.t ('Neb-mehyt') is nb + the goddess name Mḥy.t. The consonants could be made to fit, but the words are nb + Mehyt, not nbw + m + ḥꜣ.t. Record 1 also splits the sounds as 'em' + 'hat', not 'mehyt'. Different words, so C2 is a different name. C3 Nb=j-m-jꜥḥ ('Nebi-em-jah') is nb=j + m + jꜥḥ 'moon'. It has no t, so record 1's final t would render a consonant C3 lacks. Its words also differ. No match. Review X fails because it treats 'Nub' and 'hat' as mere added vowels on nb and ht, which ignores the word identity.\"}\n```"
